        "rapidmlx/qwen3.6:35b-a3b",
        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "markdown-it's block parser recognises link reference definitions such as [foo]: /url \"title\" (src/rules_block/reference.ts) and records them in env.references, but it leaves nothing in the token stream, so a plugin cannot tell where a definition stood in the source. Make every definition the block parser recognises push a token of type reference_definition at the place the definition takes in the block token stream, with nesting 0, an empty tag, hidden true, block true, the current block level, map set to [first line, line after the last line] of the definition (a definition spread over several lines covers all of them), and meta built the way the link and image tokens that use a reference already build theirs: an object with no prototype (Object.create(null)) whose label is the normalized label, the key env.references uses (so [Foo Bar]: /x gives the label \"FOO BAR\"). A later duplicate definition, whose label env.references already holds, still gets its own token, while env.references keeps the first definition as it does today. Existing plugins that walk block tokens do not expect the new type, so also add a core rule named strip_references that runs right after the block core rule and before inline and removes these tokens, and enable it in all three presets (default, commonmark and zero), so that by default md.parse returns exactly the token stream it returns today. Disabling the rule (md.core.ruler.disable('strip_references'), or md.disable('strip_references')) keeps the tokens, and md.enable('strip_references') removes them again. Hidden tokens render to nothing, but the renderer decides line feeds from the tokens directly before and after a block tag (Renderer#renderToken in src/renderer.ts), so today a kept reference_definition token would change the HTML around neighbouring blocks. With the tokens kept, md.render and md.renderer.render must produce exactly the HTML that the default configuration produces: for example, with the rule disabled, md.render('1. [foo]: /url') must still be '<ol>\\n<li></li>\\n</ol>\\n'. What must not change: with the default configuration of every preset, md.parse(src, env) yields the same tokens with the same fields, env.references gets the same entries, and md.render gives the same HTML as today for every input, with any options; the CommonMark spec tests keep passing; and disabling the rule on one MarkdownIt instance does not affect another. The source is TypeScript under src/ (core rules in src/rules_core/, registered in src/parser_core.ts; presets in src/presets/; the renderer in src/renderer.ts), run by Node directly; the tests are node:test files under test/, with parser behaviour in test/markdown-it/misc.test.mjs. Add regression tests for this behaviour to the project's existing test suite. The project's tests run with npm test (eslint, the build, tsc --noEmit, then node --test over test/cmspec, test/markdown-it and test/build).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm test) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes."
      ],
